	abstract = {In this article, the effect of uncertainty on economic growth in Latin America between 1960 and 2016 is explored. It is considered that uncertainty maintains a correlation with inflation and with the volatility of three macroeconomic variables: inflation rate, GDP and actual exchange rate. Empirical evidence indicates that uncertainty is detrimental to growth, especially when it reaches high levels. In accordance with the existing consensus in the literature, the results seem to indicate that macroeconomic instability has been a major obstacle, which explains the low economic performance of the region. Economic policy recommendations include tightening countercyclical policies and stabilizing prices and output fluctuations. © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
