	abstract = {In recent years, a set of empirical works documented the decrease in the intensity of the transfer of the nominal exchange rate to domestic prices in peripheral or developing countries. In accordance with the traditional hypothesis, this trend - verified in the last two decades - was interpreted as the result of greater credibility in monetary policy, following the implementation of formal inflation targeting regimes. This paper presents an alternative interpretation, related to the structuralist tradition, which considers other transformations registered in parallel to the establishment of inflation target regimes such as the generalized flexibility of the labor market. From empirical estimates for a set of peripheral countries relative to the 1994-2016 period, a strong statistical association was found between the intensity of the exchange rate transfer and a weakening of labor institutions. © 2019 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
